-- title:
--   Admissible triples have a uniformly large rank-two Fourier coefficient
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Sign matrices are Boolean $m\times m$ matrices; $\mathrm{row}(\sigma)$ and $\mathrm{col}(\tau)$ are the matrices with entries $\sigma_i$ and $\tau_j$, $\oplus$ is XOR, and $\mathrm{sgn}(0)=1$, $\mathrm{sgn}(1)=-1$. A triple $(\Psi,f,g)$ with $\Psi$ on sign matrices and $f,g$ on $\{0,1\}^m$ is scalar admissible with parameters $a,b$ if: all three are odd and bounded by $4$; $\hat f(e_i)=a$ and $\hat g(e_j)=b$ for all unit vectors; and the row and column identities hold, $\mathbb E_\sigma\mathrm{sgn}(\sigma_i)\Psi(\mathrm{row}(\sigma)\oplus S)=a\,g(S_{i\cdot})$ and $\mathbb E_\tau\mathrm{sgn}(\tau_j)\Psi(\mathrm{col}(\tau)\oplus S)=b\,f(S_{\cdot j})$. Let $d(m)=\lceil2\log_2m\rceil$. Let $m$ satisfy $4\le d(m)<m$, and let $a,b>0$ with
--   $$
--   80\,d(m)\bigl(d(m)+1\bigr)^2\le\min(ma,mb).
--   $$
--   Then there is $c>0$ such that for every scalar admissible triple $(\Psi,f,g)$ with parameters $a,b$ some frequency $P$ of $\mathbb F_2$-rank at least $2$ has $|\hat\Psi(P)|\ge c$.
--
--   This coefficient target is the scalar heart of the proof that $X$ has nontrivial type.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/openai/math/blob/1581d4cbdd69a0faf34b2089614fe2a814316a6a/lean/OAI/Analysis/Cotype/CoefficientCompactness.lean#L74-L92

